<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Strategos</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Strategos is a Greek word meaning general, used in the Hellenistic world for military and civil governors. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapies</a> were administered by governors called strategoi or satraps. Accounts of the Seleucid Empire describe them as appointed by the king.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/strategos.jpg" alt="Strategos" width="277" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Copy of Kresilas · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pericles_Pio-Clementino_Inv269_n2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Strategos, plural strategoi, is a compound of the Greek stratos, army, and agos, leader. In Classical Athens the office existed already in the 6th century BCE but took its best known form with the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleisthenes">Cleisthenes</a> in 501 BCE, which set up a board of ten strategoi elected annually, one from each tribe, in place of the polemarchos as senior commander.</p><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marathon">Battle of Marathon</a> in 490 BCE they decided strategy by majority vote, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>. In the early 5th century many combined military and political roles, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimon">Cimon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a>, though their power came from personal charisma. Athenians watched them closely: Pericles was removed from office and fined in 430 BCE, and six of the eight strategoi at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-arginusae">Arginusae</a> in 406 BCE were removed and condemned to death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> was elected strategos autokrator, with full powers, of the Hellenic League, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> succeeded him in the post. In the Aetolian and Achaean Leagues the annually elected strategos was both chief of civil government and supreme military commander; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aratus-of-sicyon">Aratus of Sicyon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philopoemen">Philopoemen of Megalopolis</a> were repeatedly re-elected in the Achaean League. In Ptolemaic Egypt the strategoi at first commanded Greek military colonists but by the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-ii-philadelphus">Ptolemy II Philadelphus</a> were heads of provincial administration, and Ptolemy V Epiphanes created the office of epistrategos to oversee them.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> the strategos at first rendered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> magister militum. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">theme system</a> of the 7th century the strategoi combined military command with civil government of a theme, and in the 8th century they often rebelled against the emperor. The themes were split up and the strategoi multiplied, from 18 around 842 to almost 90 in the Escorial Taktikon of about 971 to 975. By the 13th century the word meant simply a general.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Greek historians used strategos for the Roman praetor, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-the-apostles">Acts of the Apostles</a> uses it for the magistrates of Philippi. In the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> the strategus was a local governor commanding the militia. In the modern Hellenic Army stratigós is the highest officer rank, and most other Greek general ranks derive from the word.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Strategos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At first the league had two generals and a common secretary; from 255 BCE a single general (<b>strategos</b>) led it, the first being Margus of Keryneia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaean-league">Achaean League · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Philip was elected archon of the Thessalian League, took Phocis's two seats on the Amphictyonic Council, and in 337 BCE became hegemon and <b>strategos</b> autokrator of the League of Corinth, of which his kingdom was officially not a member.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Each of the roughly forty nomes was run by a <b>strategos</b>, and village scribes registered land, people and animals for tax.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Each nome, or province, was governed by a <b>strategos</b>, with an oikonomos for its economic affairs, and each village had its chief administrator, the komarches.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ptolemaic-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ptolemaic Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>The league had a federal assembly, council and elected general (<b>strategos</b>), and struck federal coins.</blockquote><a href="/state/acarnanian-league">Acarnanian League</a></li><li><blockquote>The league had a general assembly of all citizens meeting at Thermon and elsewhere, a large council and an annual general (<b>strategos</b>) assisted by a committee, the apokletoi.</blockquote><a href="/state/aetolian-league">Aetolian League</a></li></ul></section>
